# NOTE: every credential below is a placeholder. Put your own values in .env, which is
# gitignored -- never here. A real password in a committed example is published the moment the
# repository is, and it stays in the history after it is edited out.
# Local development environment. Copy to .env, which is gitignored:
#
#   make env        (copies this file if .env does not exist yet)
#
# The Makefile loads .env and exports every name in it, so `make run` needs no typing
# and no shell-specific syntax. .env is read by make, not by the binary: the service
# still takes its configuration from the process environment only, so nothing here
# changes how it behaves in a deployment.
#
# Values are written without surrounding quotes. make passes the rest of the line
# verbatim, quotes included, so a quoted DSN arrives with the quotes still attached
# and fails as a connection error.

# The two runtime roles. Distinct on purpose: organization_app carries row-level
# security by app.tenant_id, organization_provider_app carries cross-Tenant scope.
# Using one for both would make every RLS test pass for the wrong reason.
ORGANIZATION_TENANT_DATABASE_URL=postgres://organization_app:CHANGEME@localhost:5432/organization_control_dev?sslmode=disable
ORGANIZATION_PROVIDER_DATABASE_URL=postgres://organization_provider_app:CHANGEME@localhost:5432/organization_control_dev?sslmode=disable

# The resolver's own credential. Closing a dead letter is what makes the frontier stop
# reporting a security debt, and it is the only act in this service with a role of its own.
# Startup refuses this if it matches either DSN above: one credential doing both replay and
# closure would make the evidence between them decorative.
ORGANIZATION_RESOLUTION_DATABASE_URL=postgres://organization_resolution_app:CHANGEME@localhost:5432/organization_control_dev?sslmode=disable

# Points at cmd/organization-devissuer (make issuer). Compared for exact equality,
# so a trailing slash here refuses every token and reads as a permissions bug.
ORGANIZATION_TOKEN_ISSUER=http://127.0.0.1:8098
ORGANIZATION_JWKS_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8098/certs
# This service's keyless resource registration, never a client that authenticates (STD-IAM-002 §3.1).
ORGANIZATION_TOKEN_AUDIENCE=organization-control-api

# A caller is read from the token's standard claims and this service's own records, never from a
# role: no setting names a claim or a role (TDD-organization-control-001 §Caller Authority).
#
# The consumer's own credential. Set, it enables consumer authority: a workload registered with its
# principal_id may perform the authoritative fresh check without provider authority. A consumer runs
# as organization_consumer_rt, never as the provider role; startup refuses a DSN shared with another
# pool. The dev issuer mints one with role=consumer.
ORGANIZATION_CONSUMER_DATABASE_URL=postgres://organization_consumer_app:CHANGEME@localhost:5432/organization_control_dev?sslmode=disable

# Delivering to foundation-reference running beside this service (TDD-organization-control-005
# §Technical Context). Uncomment to run its dispatcher in this process. The workload token comes from
# the dev issuer's client credentials grant, which needs the three ORGANIZATION_DEVISSUER_ lines below
# (the issuer reads them; the service ignores them). The key files are yours to generate, RSA of at
# least 3072 bits, under the gitignored deploy/dev/keys/ -- never commit one:
#   openssl genpkey -algorithm RSA -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:3072 -out deploy/dev/keys/workload.pem
#   openssl pkey -in deploy/dev/keys/workload.pem -pubout -out deploy/dev/keys/workload.pub.pem
# foundation-reference's REFERENCE_DELIVERY_PRINCIPAL_ID must equal the issuer's principal_id, whose
# default is 55555555-5555-4555-8555-55555555550b.
# ORGANIZATION_DELIVERY_TARGETS=foundation-reference=http://127.0.0.1:8096/v1/deliveries
# ORGANIZATION_DISPATCH_DATABASE_URL=postgres://organization_dispatch_app:CHANGEME@localhost:5432/organization_control_dev?sslmode=disable
# ORGANIZATION_WORKLOAD_CLIENT_ID=organization-control-workload
# ORGANIZATION_WORKLOAD_KEY_FILE=deploy/dev/keys/workload.pem
# ORGANIZATION_WORKLOAD_TOKEN_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8098/token
# ORGANIZATION_DELIVERY_INTERVAL=500ms
# ORGANIZATION_DELIVERY_IDLE_INTERVAL=1s
# ORGANIZATION_DEVISSUER_CLIENT_ID=organization-control-workload
# ORGANIZATION_DEVISSUER_CLIENT_PUBLIC_KEY_FILE=deploy/dev/keys/workload.pub.pem

ORGANIZATION_LISTEN_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1:8099

# The OpenTelemetry Collector's OTLP/HTTP base URL. Unset, nothing is exported and startup says so;
# the enforcement alerts in observability/alerts then fire EnforcementTelemetryAbsent.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:4318

# Optional. The defaults are in internal/config/config.go; these are here so the
# knobs are discoverable rather than because local development needs them changed.
# ORGANIZATION_TOKEN_MAX_SKEW=60s
# ORGANIZATION_READINESS_TIMEOUT=2s
# ORGANIZATION_PROVISIONING_TIMEOUT=30m
# ORGANIZATION_PROVISIONING_RECONCILE_INTERVAL=15m
# ORGANIZATION_INVITATION_SWEEP_INTERVAL=1h
# ORGANIZATION_TENANT_NAME_MAX=120

# Integration tests connect as the owner rather than as either runtime role, because
# they create and drop schema, then reconnect as each runtime role to prove row-level
# security from the outside. Only the tests read these four.
#
# With TEST_DATABASE_URL unset the integration tests skip rather than fail, which is
# why REQUIRE_INTEGRATION exists: `make test-integration` sets it to 1 so a skip is a
# failure. A green suite that silently ran no integration test is the failure mode
# both variables exist to prevent.
TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:CHANGEME@localhost:5432/organization_control_dev?sslmode=disable
TEST_RUNTIME_PASSWORD=CHANGEME
TEST_PROVIDER_PASSWORD=CHANGEME
TEST_DISPATCH_PASSWORD=CHANGEME
TEST_RESOLUTION_PASSWORD=resolution
TEST_CONSUMER_PASSWORD=consumer

# Keeps the Go build cache off a full C: drive.
GOTMPDIR=D:\Ansha\.gotmp
